  "controls": [
    {
      "id": "2610.23665",
      "title": "TriRouter: Sparse-to-Dense Electrode-Space Routing for Robust EEG Motor Imagery Decoding",
      "abstract": "Motor imagery brain-computer interfaces require reliable decoding from multichannel EEG, but practical acquisition often yields sparse or corrupted electrode montages. We propose TriRouter, an electrode-space routing framework with discriminative-preservation, mask-robustness, and latent-compensation paths. A validation-calibrated router uses observable channel availability to allocate path contributions, preserving complete-channel discrimination while increasing representation recovery under severe missingness. In BNCI2014-001, TriRouter outperforms standard public backbones and a dynamic-spatial-filtering corrupted-EEG baseline. It improves accuracy over the strongest corrupted-EEG baseline by 3.68, 4.02, and 6.46 percentage points at nominal 25%, 50%, and 75% missing settings, and remains effective under structured regional missingness and additional low-channel dataset evaluation.",
      "subjects": "Machine Learning (cs.LG) ; Signal Processing (eess.SP)",
      "v1_utc": "2026-10-16T15:59:00+00:00",
      "v1_local": "2026-10-16T23:59:00+08:00",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23665"
    },
    {
      "id": "2610.23668",
      "title": "TUPLE-Bench: Evaluating and Mitigating Privacy-Utility Trade-offs in Typed Decision Models",
      "abstract": "Typed decision models often return more than a selected category. They also expose probabilities for each candidate and, sometimes, a separate confidence score. These fields support downstream decisions but may reveal information beyond a recipient's authorization. We introduce TUPLE-Bench (Typed decision models' Utility and Privacy Leakage Evaluation Benchmark), a benchmark of paired synthetic cases in which recipients may learn a coarse operational category but not its fine-grained cause. Across nine models, instructions to withhold the cause fail to protect it: 91.88%-99.93% of responses reveal the cause through direct selection or residual scores. Even when causes are excluded from the candidate set, full outputs enable 62.85%-75.35% recovery on held-out cases, compared with 51.04%-57.64% using only the selected category (50% chance). Full outputs also reduce mean downstream cost in all 27 model-task comparisons. However, confidence fields increase leakage without consistently reducing cost. We further introduce workflow-constrained privacy quantization (WCPQ), which reduces secret recovery while preserving the behavior and cost of specified downstream workflows. Our findings highlight the need to audit decision interfaces field by field and workflow by workflow.",
      "subjects": "Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)",
      "v1_utc": "2026-10-16T16:00:57+00:00",
      "v1_local": "2026-10-17T00:00:57+08:00",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23668"
    },
    {
      "id": "2610.23856",
      "title": "Learning to Correct Geometry in Generated Videos",
      "abstract": "Generated videos often lose geometric consistency under camera motion: structures warp under viewpoint change, parallax becomes inconsistent, and smooth camera movement may collapse into shot-like transitions. We argue that this failure is largely a data problem. In web-scale video corpora, most visible motion comes from dynamic objects captured by static or weakly moving cameras, leaving only sparse supervision for viewpoint-induced appearance changes. To address this, we propose Video Geometry Corrector (VGC), a post-hoc video-to-video model trained on synthetic paired supervision. Starting from real videos with diverse camera motion and pose annotations, we construct VG-Pairs with a synthetic paired-supervision data generation pipeline. Each video clip is treated as a geometry-consistent target and paired with a distorted counterpart synthesized by a pretrained image-to-video inpainting model using trajectory-adaptive keyframes. VGC learns to map the distorted input to the geometry-consistent target without modifying the source generator, and uses optical flow as an auxiliary cue to help disentangle camera motion from object motion. Experiments on pose-conditioned and prompt-driven benchmarks show that VGC improves geometric consistency across multiple source generators, and scaling to a larger backbone further extends the framework toward more general video refinement.",
      "subjects": "Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)",
      "v1_utc": "2026-10-16T17:59:55+00:00",
      "v1_local": "2026-10-17T01:59:55+08:00",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23856"
    },
    {
      "id": "2610.23469",
      "title": "When Evidence Vanishes: Distilling Visual-Evidence Dependency in Medical Vision-Language Models",
      "abstract": "Compact medical vision-language models (VLMs) can answer clinical questions correctly without reliably using the image, relying instead on question wording or learned priors. Standard knowledge distillation copies a teacher's answer preference on the original image, but it leaves unchecked whether that preference is conditional on the visual evidence required by the question. We study this missing target as medical visual evidence dependency: support for the correct answer should decrease when the matched evidence is absent. We introduce Evidence-Conditioned Dependency Distillation (ECDD), which pairs each training image with a control matched to the evidence level and distills the teacher's drop in answer margin across the pair. A lower bound objective transfers positive dependency by penalizing only shortfalls, while original-image supervision preserves task accuracy. The same formulation applies when the evidence is a queried clinical region, a surgical instrument, or whole-image visual content. Across three medical VQA settings, ECDD keeps original-image accuracy competitive with distillation and alignment baselines while making answer support more sensitive to evidence removal. Under the DME headline objective, for example, the positive-case margin response to target rather than random masking rises from 0.786 with KD to 2.023 with ECDD, while original-image accuracy differs by 0.11 points. These results suggest that medical VLM distillation should transfer both the teacher's answer preference and its dependence on visual evidence.",
      "subjects": "Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)",
      "v1_utc": "2026-10-16T13:48:30+00:00",
      "v1_local": "2026-10-16T21:48:30+08:00",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23469"
    }
  ],
